## Expected Behavior

Go-to-definition on a method with multiple plausible targets returns ALL
of them (LSP `GotoDefinitionResponse::Array` — editors show a peek list):

- `i.Go()` on an interface-typed receiver → the interface's declaration
  PLUS every workspace class implementing that interface, at each
  class's `Go` definition.
- A base-class method overridden in derived classes, called through a
  base-typed receiver → base definition plus overrides.
- Free-function / method OVERLOAD sets where the call site cannot be
  narrowed to one signature → every overload ("anything that has
  overloads like this or implementations or whatever should work like
  this" — Max).

## Actual Behavior

Definition returns a single location. For interface methods this is
deliberately pinned today: `src/server/navigation.rs:778`
(`goto_definition_interface_method_on_an_interface_typed_receiver`)
asserts "must land on the interface declaration, not the class
implementation" — that contract changes to "interface declaration FIRST,
implementations after it" and the test updates with it.

## Design notes

- Ordering: the declaration the receiver's static type owns comes first
  (what today's behavior returns), implementations/overrides after —
  deterministic order (workspace file order) so tests can pin it.
- Implementations are found from the workspace symbol table's
  inheritance records (the checker already walks base/interface chains
  for member resolution — reuse that, not a textual search).
- Only the dependency-closure-complete workspace is authoritative for
  "every implementer" (same fence as GH #58 / workspace
  NoMatchingSymbol); with an incomplete closure still list what is
  known — extra locations are suggestions, not diagnostics, so the
  authority fence can be looser here.
- Consider also wiring `textDocument/implementation` (the dedicated LSP
  request) with the implementations-only list while in there; definition
  returning the union is the ticket's bar because that is the gesture
  people actually use.

## Generalize (repo convention)

- interface with one implementer / several implementers / zero (interface
  location only).
- diamond: class implements two interfaces sharing a method name.
- override chains: base virtual + two derived overrides.
- overloaded free functions: unresolvable call site lists all overloads;
  a RESOLVABLE call site keeps the single precise target (negative
  control — do not regress precise navigation).
- accessor pair from the get_/set_ ticket, once both land: interface
  declares `get_ID`, classes implement it.

